## Build & Run

- **Build**: `./gradlew build` (or `gradlew.bat build` on Windows)
- **Test**: `./gradlew test`. The integration tests start PostgreSQL with Testcontainers, so they need Docker and nothing else
- **Database**: `task db-up` at the repository root starts the workspace's PostgreSQL (`docker compose up -d db`)
- **Run with the dev identity**: `task {{ app_id }}:dev`, which sets `SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE=local` and runs `./gradlew bootRun`
- **Run without a profile**: `./gradlew bootRun`. No dev identity exists then, and every token is rejected until `spring.security.oauth2.resourceserver.jwt.issuer-uri` names your identity provider and `spring.security.oauth2.resourceserver.jwt.audiences` names this API (the app refuses to start with a provider and no audience, and rejects a token whose `aud` is missing or another API's)
- **Port**: {{ port }} (configurable with the `PORT` environment variable)
- **Health**: `GET /actuator/health` is open and reports the database

## The slice

| Route | Profile | What it does |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `POST /api/dev-identity/token` | `local` only | Signs a development JWT for an email and display name |
| `GET /api/dev-identity/jwks` | `local` only | Publishes the dev identity's public key as a JWKS document, so another local service (an agent service) verifies the tokens with no shared secret |
| `GET /api/me` | all | Returns the profile of the token's subject, created on the first call |

Files, each under `src/main/kotlin/{{ app_package_path }}/`:

- `modules/users/` - `controller/MeController.kt`, `service/UserService.kt`, `repository/UserRepository.kt`, `model/User.kt`, `dto/UserProfileResponse.kt`
- `modules/devidentity/` - the token controller, its service and the loopback policy
- `bootstrap/SecurityConfig.kt` - every route needs a bearer JWT except `/actuator/health`, the token route and the JWKS route
- `modules/devidentity/service/DevIdentityJwksService.kt` - the public key set; only the public half leaves the service
- `bootstrap/DevIdentityConfig.kt`, `bootstrap/DevIdentityGuard.kt` - the in-memory signing key and the startup guard, both `local` only
- `src/main/resources/db/migration/V1__users.sql` - the `users` table

## The dev identity is not authentication

- It is **local development sign-in**. Never present it, or build a feature on it, as complete authentication.
- It exists only under `@Profile("local")`. The default profile answers `POST /api/dev-identity/token` and `GET /api/dev-identity/jwks` with 404, and `DefaultProfileIntegrationTest` proves it.
- It signs JWTs (`iss=prism-dev-identity`, 15 minutes) with an RSA key generated in memory at startup and never written anywhere. No JWT secret exists in this app.
- It accepts loopback requests only, for the token route and the JWKS route: the peer address and the server name must be loopback, and a request with a forwarding header is refused.
- The JWKS route publishes the public key only. Never add a private member to it.
- Startup fails when `local` is active and a real identity provider is configured (`spring.security.oauth2.resourceserver.jwt.issuer-uri` or its siblings).
- Startup fails outside `local` when a provider is configured without `spring.security.oauth2.resourceserver.jwt.audiences` (`AudienceGuard`), and `AudienceCheckingJwtDecoder` rejects a token with no or a foreign `aud`.
- The generated `docker-compose.yml` sets no profile. Docs and the `dev` task set `local` explicitly. Never add `local` to a shared or deployed environment.

Replace it with your identity provider before the app is exposed: the `security-auth` skill has the steps. Do not weaken a guard above to make a test pass.

## Tests

- `*IntegrationTest` classes boot the app against a Testcontainers PostgreSQL (`support/PostgresTestConfiguration.kt`): `DefaultProfileIntegrationTest` (no profile) and `LocalProfileIntegrationTest` (`local`).
- `DevIdentityStartupGuardTest` proves that `local` plus a configured issuer fails startup; `AudienceEnforcementTest` and `AudienceIntegrationTest` prove the audience rules.
- `OpenApiContractTest` checks that `shared/api-contracts/openapi.yml` defines the slice's operations.
- Service and policy unit tests use MockK and plain JUnit; they need no Docker.
- The integration tests use Testcontainers, not the compose database, so a test run never touches your development data and CI needs no service container.

## Critical Rules

### Forbidden Patterns

- `entity -> DTO` imports (entities never depend on API DTOs)
- `controller -> repository` imports (controllers call services, never repositories directly)
- `runBlocking` in MVC controllers
- A signing key, JWT secret or token written to disk, a log or the repository

### Gotchas

- **Jackson 3.x split namespace**: core annotations stay at `com.fasterxml.jackson.annotation.*`, runtime classes moved to `tools.jackson.*`. There is NO `tools.jackson.annotation` package.
- **Inject ObjectMapper**: Spring beans must inject `tools.jackson.databind.ObjectMapper`, never create standalone `jacksonObjectMapper()`.
- **File granularity**: keep each DTO, entity, enum, and reusable value object in its own Kotlin file. Avoid `*Dtos.kt` aggregator files and avoid nesting reusable enums inside entity files.
- **One schema per app**: tables live in the `{{ app_id | replace('-', '_') }}` schema (`DATABASE_SCHEMA`), so several backends can share the workspace database.

## Architecture

```
src/main/kotlin/{{ app_package_path }}/
  {{ app_module_name }}Application  - Spring Boot entry point
  bootstrap/                        - Framework wiring: security, dev identity, properties
  modules/                          - Feature modules
    users/                          - GET /api/me: controller/, dto/, model/, repository/, service/
    devidentity/                    - The local dev identity: controller/, dto/, error/, model/, service/
  shared/                           - Cross-cutting code: audit/, error/, exception/, model/
```

A module holds the folders it needs of `controller/`, `dto/`, `error/`, `model/`, `repository/` and `service/`.

